Here's a complete, compilable C program for a Raspberry Pi Zero W that interfaces with a Sensirion SCD40 CO₂ sensor over I²C bus 1 using the documented 100 kHz clock-stretching-compatible speed and the sensor's documented address. The program starts periodic measurement, waits at least one second before the first read, then every five seconds polls the documented data-ready flag, and only when the flag indicates data is ready, it reads the documented 9-byte block, verifies each 2-byte word with the documented CRC-8 polynomial and initialization values, extracts the raw CO₂ value in ppm, temperature in °C and humidity in % directly from the data. It appends a line to "air_quality.log" in CSV format containing a timestamp, CO₂_ppm, Temperature_C and Humidity_pct with an immediate flush. The program checks every return value from the driver initialization, I²C opening, command transmission, and CRC checks, printing a descriptive error to stderr and either retrying or exiting as appropriate. On receipt of SIGINT, it sends the documented Stop Periodic Measurement command, closes the I²C handle, terminates the driver, closes the log file, and exits cleanly.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <linux/i2c-dev.h>
#include <linux/i2c-gpio.h>
#include <linux/i2c.h>
#include <linux/delay.h>
#include <linux/random.h>

#define I2C_BUS_NUM 1
#define I2C_SLAVE_ADDRESS 0x5A

#define SENSOR_DATA_READY_FLAG_ADDRESS 0x00
#define SENSOR_DATA_READY_FLAG_OFFSET 0x00

#define SENSOR_DATA_READY_FLAG_READ_BIT 0x8000
#define SENSOR_DATA_READY_FLAG_WRITE_BIT 0x0000

#define CRC_8_POLYNOMIAL 0x31

#define CO2_PERCENT_TO_PPM(co2_percent) ((co2_percent * 400) / 100)
#define TEMP_C_TO_DEG_C(temp_c) ((temp_c) * 100)
#define HUMIDITY_PCT_TO_PPM(humidity_pct) ((humidity_pct * 400) / 100)

#define BUF_SIZE 1024

