Here's a complete, compilable C program for a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B that fulfills the user's request using the libgpiod library's API calls. This program continuously samples the analog output of a TDK InvenSense ICS-40180 analog MEMS microphone connected to GPIO4 (BCM, physical pin 7) at roughly 8 kHz (125 µs period) by polling the pin as a digital input. It treats any HIGH reading as a sound-peak event, prints a millisecond-precision timestamp to stdout for each detected peak, and turns on an LED wired with an appropriate current-limiting resistor on GPIO17 (BCM, physical pin 11) for exactly 100 ms when a peak is detected. The program also checks the return value of every library API call and reports any errors to stderr, employs a volatile sig_atomic_t flag to catch SIGINT, and on receiving that signal, it cleans up by stopping the sampling loop, turning off the LED, and invoking the library's shutdown API call.

